# Target C-II — near-floor three-spoke pentagons carry exact contact flexes

**Date:** 2026-09-18
**Status:** proof-only structural refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged
**Scope:** terminal full-core Type-II FREE subsequences in the concrete low-detour near-`t` window
**Depends on:** the certified `0.4155508927...D` near-orbit pentagon floor; the pentagonal-hole strengthening of the double-ear theorem; terminal `t`-stability; the exact stopped-train endpoint multigraph; `P_5-F_5^{full}<=2S`; the five-root two-ear closure lemma; the no-return-to-one-face train theorem; the train-angle/face-period representation
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_t_near_floor_three_spoke_exact_flex_2026_09_18.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current double-ear theorem now correctly identifies every fully-rhombic pentagon
as a five-edge hole of the full strict-rhombus complex. Thus its five train directions
are constrained by a genuine hole-period equation. This note isolates a positive-
density branch where that equation can be solved exactly rather than estimated by a
global kernel dimension.

A near-orbit fully-rhombic pentagon with at least three incident trains running to the
actual outer face has a one-parameter **exact** contact-preserving deformation obtained
by varying only three of those outer-reaching train directions and maintaining the
pentagon/hole closure equation. One of the two near-unit ears is nonstationary along that
curve, so the parameter can be oriented to shorten it. Different such pentagons use
disjoint pentagon-to-outer train coordinates, hence these local curves form a product
family and one near-unit ear per pentagon can be shortened simultaneously.

In the concrete window

\[
\limsup t_D\le1.959952849540479\ldots,
\qquad
\limsup{b-P_F\over D}\le10^{-5},
\tag{TS.1}
\]

the transport ledger forces the trichotomy

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{
 {S\over D},\ {L_O\over D},\ {Y^{near}_{3O}\over D}
\right\}
\ge
0.045152736769091\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{TS.2}
\]

where `S` is the singleton stopped-train count, `L_O` is the outer-to-outer stopped-
train count, and `Y^{near}_{3O}` is the number of near-orbit fully-rhombic pentagons
with at least three pentagon-to-outer trains.

Thus, away from the singleton branch, either at least `0.0451D` exact global O--O
multishear coordinates already exist, or at least `0.0451D` independent exact
pentagon-spoke contact-flex coordinates exist and can be oriented to shorten that many
near-unit ear inequalities simultaneously. The remaining obstruction on the latter
branch is the active diameter system, not contact closure.

---

## 1. Scalar window and the bounded-to-bounded train budget

Retain the notation of the terminal `t`-gap and near-contact notes. Put

\[
s_2=\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}},
\qquad
g=0.007707,
\qquad
M_*=4s_2+2g,
\tag{1.1}
\]

\[
e_*={M_*-\pi\over2}
=0.341536286933018\ldots .
\tag{1.2}
\]

The current concrete window is `e=0.00669` above the old terminal baseline:

\[
e=0.00659+10^{-4}=0.00669.
\tag{1.3}
\]

After the standard subsequence extraction, write

\[
\delta=\lim{b-P_F\over D},
\qquad
r=\lim{Q-f\over D},
\qquad
a=\lim\left({\tau_D\over D}-\tau_*\right).
\tag{1.4}
\]

The exact terminal `t` decomposition gives

\[
\boxed{a+2(r+\delta)\le5e,}
\tag{1.5}
\]

with `a,r,delta>=0`. The stopped-train endpoint multigraph has bounded-to-bounded edge
count `E_BB` and outer-loop count `L_O`, with the exact identity

\[
E_{BB}-L_O={4f+Q-b\over2}.
\tag{1.6}
\]

The same terminal ledger gives

\[
{f\over D}
={M_*+2a-\delta-r\over5}+o(1),
\qquad
{b\over D}=\pi+\delta+o(1).
\tag{1.7}
\]

Substitution into (1.6) cancels `r` exactly:

\[
\boxed{
{E_{BB}\over D}
={L_O\over D}+e_*+a-\delta+o(1).
}
\tag{1.8}
\]

By (1.5),

\[
a-\delta
\le5e-2r-3\delta
\le5e.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
{E_{BB}\over D}
\le {L_O\over D}+e_*+5e+o(1).
}
\tag{1.9}
\]

Numerically,

\[
e_*+5e
=0.374986286933018\ldots .
\tag{1.10}
\]

---

## 2. Near fully-rhombic pentagons force outer spokes unless `E_BB` is large

The certified near-contact theorem gives, in (TS.1),

\[
\lim_{\theta\downarrow0}\liminf
{P_5^{near}(\theta)\over D}
\ge
N_0,
\qquad
N_0=0.415550892775345\ldots .
\tag{2.1}
\]

Let `H_theta` be the near-orbit pentagons that are also fully rhombic. The exact
singleton/full-flower inequality gives

\[
|H_\theta|
\ge P_5^{near}(\theta)-2S.
\tag{2.2}
\]

For `P in H_theta`, let `o(P)` be the number of its five distinct stopped trains whose
other terminal occurrence is the actual outer face. The five trains are distinct by
the no-return-to-one-face theorem. Therefore `5-o(P)` is the number of bounded-to-
bounded train incidences at `P`.

Every bounded-to-bounded train has only two bounded terminal occurrences, so over any
subset of bounded faces

\[
\sum_{P\in H_\theta}(5-o(P))\le2E_{BB}.
\tag{2.3}
\]

Let

\[
Y^{near}_{3O}(\theta)
:=\#\{P\in H_\theta:o(P)\ge3\}.
\tag{2.4}
\]

Pentagons outside this set contribute at most two outer incidences and pentagons inside
it at most five. Thus

\[
\sum_{P\in H_\theta}o(P)
\le2|H_\theta|+3Y^{near}_{3O}(\theta).
\tag{2.5}
\]

On the other hand, (2.3) gives

\[
\sum_{P\in H_\theta}o(P)
=5|H_\theta|-\sum_{P\in H_\theta}(5-o(P))
\ge5|H_\theta|-2E_{BB}.
\tag{2.6}
\]

Combining (2.5)--(2.6),

\[
\boxed{
Y^{near}_{3O}(\theta)
\ge |H_\theta|-{2\over3}E_{BB}.
}
\tag{2.7}
\]

Insert (1.9) and (2.2), then shrink `theta` along the usual boundary-null sequence. In
normalized form,

\[
\boxed{
{Y^{near}_{3O}\over D}
\ge
K_0
-2{S\over D}
-{2\over3}{L_O\over D}
-o(1),
}
\tag{2.8}
\]

where

\[
\boxed{
K_0
:=N_0-{2\over3}(e_*+5e)
=0.165560034820000\ldots .
}
\tag{2.9}
\]

This is stronger than intersecting the near-pentagon theorem with the transport-fan
ladder by a crude union bound. It uses the exact bounded-to-bounded transport budget.

Set

\[
c_{3O}:={3K_0\over11}
=0.045152736769091\ldots .
\tag{2.10}
\]

If all three quantities in (TS.2) were smaller than `(c_{3O}-o(1))D`, then (2.8)
would give

\[
{Y^{near}_{3O}\over D}
\ge
K_0-{8\over3}c_{3O}-o(1)
=c_{3O}-o(1),
\]

a contradiction. This proves (TS.2).

---

## 3. Local three-spoke closure has a genuine ear-shortening tangent

Fix one strict convex unit pentagon `P` counted by `Y^{near}_{3O}(theta)`, with `theta`
sufficiently small for the five-root closure theorem. Orient its boundary sides
cyclically as

\[
u_0,\ldots,u_4,
\qquad
\sum_{i=0}^4u_i=0.
\tag{3.1}
\]

The five-root theorem supplies two disjoint adjacent side pairs

\[
E_1=\{a,b\},
\qquad
E_2=\{c,d\},
\tag{3.2}
\]

whose turning angles are near `2pi/3`. Let `e` be the fifth side index.

Choose any three pentagon-to-outer train sides and call their index set `O`, `|O|=3`.
Only these three train directions will vary. Define

\[
\Phi((x_i)_{i\in O})
:=
\sum_{i\in O}R_{x_i}u_i
+
\sum_{i\notin O}u_i.
\tag{3.3}
\]

Then `Phi(0)=0`. Its derivative is

\[
D\Phi(0)\,\alpha
=J\sum_{i\in O}\alpha_i u_i.
\tag{3.4}
\]

### Lemma 3.1 — rank two

`D Phi(0)` has rank two.

Indeed three boundary side vectors of a strict convex polygon cannot all lie on one
projective line. If they did, their oriented directions would use only two antipodal
values; among three edges two would have the same oriented direction, impossible for a
strictly convex polygon whose boundary direction angle is strictly increasing. Hence
the three selected vectors span the plane.

Thus `ker D Phi(0)` is one-dimensional.

### Lemma 3.2 — the tangent cannot leave both special ears stationary

Extend `alpha` by zero outside `O`. For one special ear `{i,j}`, the exact derivative
identity is

\[
{d\over dt}|u_i+u_j|^2\Big|_{0}
=2\sin\vartheta_{ij}(\alpha_i-\alpha_j),
\qquad 0<\vartheta_{ij}<\pi.
\tag{3.5}
\]

Suppose a nonzero `alpha in ker D Phi(0)` made both special-ear derivatives zero. Then

\[
\alpha_a=\alpha_b,
\qquad
\alpha_c=\alpha_d.
\tag{3.6}
\]

Because only three of the five entries can be nonzero, there are only two essential
cases.

1. If the leftover side `e` is not selected, one ear has both selected sides and the
   other has exactly one selected side. The latter equality in (3.6) forces that
   single rate to zero; the former gives a common rate `x`. Equation (3.4) reduces to
   `x(u_i+u_j)=0` on the double-selected ear. Its diagonal is nonzero, so `x=0`.

2. If `e` is selected, either the two other selected sides lie in different ears, in
   which case (3.6) kills both and then (3.4) kills the leftover rate, or both lie in
   one ear. In the latter case (3.4) becomes

   \[
   x(u_i+u_j)+z u_e=0.
   \tag{3.7}
   \]

   For the five-root pattern, the projective direction of the limiting ear diagonal
   `u_i+u_j` is separated from the limiting leftover-side direction by exactly
   `pi/3`. Since equal-length vector sums point along the angular bisector, under the
   existing orbit tolerance their projective separation is at least
   `pi/3-2theta>0`. The five-root hypothesis `10 sin(theta/2)<1` already implies
   `theta<pi/6`. Hence the two vectors in (3.7) are not parallel, forcing `x=z=0`.

In every case `alpha=0`, contrary to choice. Therefore every nonzero tangent to the
three-spoke closure curve changes at least one special ear to first order. Reversing
`alpha` if necessary makes that derivative strictly negative.

The finite sixth-root patterns and all ten choices of three variable sides are checked
by the companion certificate through the equivalent stacked-rank test.

---

## 4. The infinitesimal tangent integrates to an exact contact deformation

By Lemma 3.1 and the implicit-function theorem, the zero set

\[
\Phi^{-1}(0)
\tag{4.1}
\]

is a smooth one-dimensional curve through the origin. Choose its orientation using
Lemma 3.2. Along one side of this curve, one of the two near-unit ear diagonals
strictly decreases while the pentagon remains exactly closed and all five side lengths
remain exactly one.

This local angle curve is already enough to remove the contact-side locking systems
for the selected three trains:

- `P` is itself a five-edge rhombic hole, and `Phi=0` is exactly its vector period /
  exceptional-face closure equation;
- each selected train has terminal faces `P` and the actual outer face, so it occurs in
  no other bounded nonquadrilateral face or rhombic-hole boundary;
- every rhombus traversed by a selected train closes identically for arbitrary choices
  of its two train direction vectors.

Thus no additional rhombic-hole or bounded-exceptional-face equation is left to impose
on this local curve. The large global period-kernel estimate from the double-ear note
is not needed on this outer-spoke subfamily.

---

## 5. Different three-spoke pentagons give a product family

Let `C` be any collection of pentagons counted by `Y^{near}_{3O}(theta)`. For each
`P in C`, choose three of its pentagon-to-outer trains.

The selected train-coordinate triples for distinct pentagons are disjoint. A complete
stopped train joining `P` to the outer face has exactly one bounded terminal occurrence,
so it cannot also be a pentagon-to-outer train for another bounded pentagon.

Consequently the closure maps `Phi_P` depend on disjoint train-angle variables. Their
one-dimensional solution curves therefore form a local product manifold of dimension
`|C|`.

Assign the resulting unit vector to every contact edge in each varied train and leave
all other contact-edge vectors fixed. Every bounded face walk has zero vector period:

- selected pentagons close by construction;
- unselected bounded exceptional faces contain no varied terminal train side;
- rhombi close identically by opposite-edge cancellation.

The bounded face walks generate the cycle space of each plane contact component, so
this unit edge-vector assignment integrates to actual vertex positions, unique up to a
translation on each contact component. Contact-isolated source points may be held
fixed.

For each finite source, all old noncontact distances are strictly greater than one.
Continuity therefore supplies a neighborhood of the product origin in which every old
noncontact remains strictly greater than one. Taking the parameters smaller still
keeps every chosen shrinking ear above one. Hence this is an **exact finite
same-cardinality unit-separated deformation of the full source preserving every old
contact**.

Because the train triples are disjoint, choose the sign of each local coordinate
independently. One special near-unit ear per pentagon then strictly shortens
simultaneously.

We have proved:

### Theorem 5.1 — exact three-spoke product flex

For every sufficiently small orbit tolerance and every collection `C` of near-orbit
fully-rhombic pentagons with at least three pentagon-to-outer stopped trains, the full
source has a local exact contact-preserving deformation family of dimension at least
`|C|`. The coordinates can be oriented so that at least one of the two five-root ears
at **every** pentagon in `C` strictly shortens for positive parameter.

No source-vertex matching, rhombic period kernel, or bounded exceptional-face closure
matrix is needed.

---

## 6. Concrete near-floor package

Combine Theorem 5.1 with (TS.2). In the window (TS.1), at least one of the following
holds:

1. **singleton branch**
   \[
   S\ge(0.0451527-o(1))D;
   \tag{6.1}
   \]

2. **outer-loop branch**
   \[
   L_O\ge(0.0451527-o(1))D;
   \tag{6.2}
   \]
   the audited arbitrary O--O multishear theorem supplies that many simultaneous exact
   global contact-shear coordinates;

3. **near three-spoke branch**
   \[
   Y^{near}_{3O}\ge(0.0451527-o(1))D;
   \tag{6.3}
   \]
   Theorem 5.1 supplies an exact contact-preserving product deformation with that many
   independent coordinates, and one near-unit ear per coordinate can be shortened
   simultaneously.

The conservative common coefficient is `0.0451`.

The third branch complements the corrected pentagonal-hole theorem. Every selected
pentagon is a genuine rhombic hole, but actual outer-reaching localization reduces its
five-edge period equation to a one-dimensional exact local flex. Its coefficient is
smaller than the raw ear/hole counts, but once it occurs the entire contact-closure
obstruction disappears on the selected coordinates.

---

## 7. Scope and remaining obstruction

1. **No diameter descent is claimed.** The exact product flex can increase active
   diameter distances. Global minimality therefore does not yet contradict (6.3).
2. **The outer-loop branch has the same issue.** Exact O--O multishears preserve every
   contact, but active diameter rows can block them.
3. **The singleton branch remains separate.** A generic singleton train need not be an
   outer-to-outer bridge and is not promoted here to an independent exact shear.
4. **Full source retained.** Contact-isolated points are not inserted into any affine
   kernel. They are held fixed and all strict pairwise slacks persist for sufficiently
   small parameters.
5. **No theorem-state promotion.** The numerical near-`t` input and several train
   dependencies remain proof-only, so no canonical claim status is changed.

The live low-detour near-floor problem is therefore reduced further. In two of the
three branches carrying at least `0.0451D` coordinates, contact preservation is exact
at finite amplitude and all rhombic/exceptional face closure has already been paid.
The next theorem should act directly on the active diameter rows of these exact
multi-parameter manifolds: either find a common diameter-nonincreasing direction, or
extract a rank/stress/ordered-incidence payment of linear size. The remaining singleton
branch needs a separate endpoint localization or bridge/cut argument.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact-flex refinement; C-II remains open.
